Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Wax Seals Bleed Diesel
- Story & Context: When municipal tax officers padlocked the canal docks at 3:15 AM, Yai Chom snapped the red wax clearance seals with an iron fish-scaler, throwing the shattered documents into an open carburetor as the market's generator roared to life. The track captures that exact ignition, capturing the sickening crunch of river mud under rubber boots and the sickening 210-BPM sub-kick that shattered every display window on the waterfront boardwalk. Over the shrieking 64th-note arpeggiator, Chom's voice crashes through a battered horn speaker: "You brought court summons to a river that drowns your paper!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric close-up shot of a crumpled government eviction notice stamped with red sealing wax sinking into oily, churning river water under the shadow of a speeding wooden boat, glowing red warning flares reflecting in the toxic ripples.

Track 2: Sompong's Dead-Letter Sloop
- Story & Context: Sompong was the canal mailman who spent forty years ferrying love letters and debt notices between stilted wooden shacks until city officials dissolved his route via text message without a single baht of pension. He delivered his entire leather mailbag to the Insurgents' boat at dawn, watching with tear-filled, bloodshot eyes as Daeng fed three decades of undelivered, moldering envelopes into the sampler's trigger-pads while an abusive arpeggio ripped through the 4:00 AM haze. In a raw, grief-scorched breakdown, Daeng screams Sompong's own terminal confession: "Thirty years of your forgotten weeping arrived this morning by boat!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A cinematic shot inside a floating market boat at dawn; weathered parchment letters with blurred ink and rusted postmarks swirling through dense humid fog around a glowing analog synthesizer rack strapped to wooden planks.

Track 3: Who Paid the Courier in Lead?
- Story & Context: After private canal security fired warning shots into the market's melon skiffs to force a buyout, the matriarchs chained twelve boats together across the estuary mouth, blasting a relentless 230-BPM war-stomp that overturned three security dinghies in the hydro-acoustic wash. By the time morning mist burned away, the river guards had abandoned their vessels on the mudbanks, leaving behind only floating tactical vests and spent shell casings. The track's triumphant, vengeful climax loops an intercepted radio transmission over a thundering wall of kick drums, with Chom chanting into the feedback: "Count your brass coins, boys, the water drinks whatever you fire!"
